    "result_text": "Done. The build passes green.\n\nI implemented the daily-limit rule by mirroring the existing `EnsureCityCapacity` pattern, keeping structural impact minimal \u2014 all new behaviour lives in small, self-contained classes (each with near-zero surrounding complexity), and the only existing file touched is the pipeline YAML (not a function, so no CC cost):\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureDailyLimit.java` \u2014 a one-method function step that counts owners with `registrationDate == today` and throws when the count reaches 100.\n- `DailyLimitExceededException.java` \u2014 checked exception.\n- `DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds 429 (`TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`) via `ProblemDetail`.\n- Escalation YAML mapping the exception to the handler.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted an `ensureDailyLimit` step (governed by `transaction`) between `ensureCityCapacity` and `save`, so an over-limit create is a 429 rather than a persisted overflow.\n\nNew owners already default `registrationDate` to today in `BuildOwner`, so the 101st create sees 100 same-day records and is rejected.",
